      <body>
         <entry xml:id="gontiyah-dlgenr"
            xml:lang="en"
            type="mainEntry"
            prev="#"
            next="#"
            source="Genesis Rabbah">
            <form type="lemma" opt="false">
               <orth type="lemma" xml:lang="jpa" opt="false" select="true">גונתיה</orth>
               <orth type="transliteration" xml:lang="fjb" opt="false">gwntyh</orth>
               <pron notation="rabbinic">gontiyah</pron>
            </form>
            <sense xml:id="gontiyah-dlgenr-1">
               <cit type="translation" xml:lang="en" subtype="sense">
                  <quote>Getae</quote>
               </cit>
               <cit type="translationEquivalent" xml:lang="grc">
                  <quote>Γέτες</quote>
               </cit>
               <cit type="example" xml:lang="jpa">
                  <quote>אמר ר' הונא עריקין הוינן מן קומיא 
                     <app>
                        <lem>גוניתיה</lem>
                        <rdg wit="#Vat1 Vat2">גונתיה</rdg>
                        <rdg wit="#Ox2 K V">גונדא</rdg>
                     </app> בהדא בטיסא דטיבריא והיה בידינו נרות, בשעה שכהים היינו יודעים שהוא יום ובשעה שמבהקין היינו יודעין שהוא לילה.</quote>
                  <bibl>
                     <ref type="witness">31,11</ref>
                     <ref type="witness">I 283: 5</ref>
                  </bibl>
                  <ref type="entry" xml:lang="jpa">בטיסא</ref>
                  <cit type="translation" xml:lang="en">
                     <quote>R. Hunaʾ said: Once we were taking refuge from the Getae in the caves of Tiberias. We had lamps with us: when they were dim, we knew that it was day, and when they shone brightly, we knew that it was night. (cf. Freedman / Simon I 244)</quote>
                  </cit>
                  <note>= <bibl type="ancient">Yalq 53 [14b: 20]</bibl> (<term xml:lang="jpa">גונדיא</term>)</note>
               </cit>
            </sense>
            <note>See <bibl type="modern">Sperber, Cards</bibl>; <bibl type="modern">Brüll (1883) 123</bibl>. However, see <bibl type="modern">AC II 41 n. 1</bibl>, tracing back to Gr. <term xml:lang="grc">γοῦνδαι</term> „troops” (same <bibl type="modern">Jastrow I 233b</bibl>; <bibl type="modern">Levy I 312a</bibl>); see also <bibl type="modern">AC II 324ab</bibl> (Lat. <term xml:lang="la">gentes</term>). Freedman / Simon: translate “[Roman] troops”, which fits better the reading of Ox 2, K and V and also <term xml:lang="jpa">גונדאי דרומאי</term> in <bibl type="ancient">bAZ 11a</bibl>. The interpretation “Goths” (MY ad loc., cf. <bibl type="modern">Krauss, LW II 170ab</bibl>) is probably secondary. Eventually related to <term xml:lang="jpa">ענתיים</term> <term xml:lang="fjb">ʿntyym</term> in <bibl type="ancient">MidrPs 25,14 [108]</bibl>, interpreted by <bibl type="modern">Sperber (1975)</bibl> as Gr. <term xml:lang="grc">Άνται</term> (Antae, a Slavonic tribe).</note>
            <ref type="bibliography">
               <bibl>
                  <title>Brüll (1883)</title>
                  <citedRange>123</citedRange>
               </bibl>
            </ref>
         </entry>
